row.cell:
Attempted kidnapping by Mexican soldiers of a deserter at Eagle Pass;
                            the trial of the officers implicated begun; translation from the Two
                            Republics inclosed.

Arrest of Manual Mejia: Genning and Bryant acquitted of the charge;
                            Governor Zulich’s assurance of justice and protection to all; the
                            governor’s letter inclosed.

Discrimination against American direct trade in the Cape Verde
                            Islands: The same duties levied on the trade of all nations;
                            contemplated reduction from 30 to 20 per cent.

Political: New Norwegian ministry; political parties; taxation heavy;
                            endeavors made to lessen Norway’s contribution towards the support of
                            the King.

Tariff laws of Sweden: Cotton, its manufactures, and coal oil exempt;
                            breadstuffs, meats, etc., tobacco, and spirits taxed; the bill is framed
                            in the interest of land-owners.

Passport for Mrs. Weiss, a native of Switzerland, whose husband, a
                            naturalized American citizen, deserted her, and who is now a pauper and
                            insane; a passport asked for her by the cantonal ministry of Zurich;
                            probability that it is intended to return her to the United States;
                            instruction requested.

Missionary schools in Syria and the Vilayet of Adana closed;
                            disposition in the empire to obstruct missionary teaching; permission
                            obtained to open the Syrian schools if the curriculum, text-books, and
                            teachers’ certificates be submitted to local authorities; missionaries
                            fail to comply with the requirements; an arrangement anticipated;
                            schools of Adana closed on account of alleged lack of authorization;
                            telegram sent by the grand vizier to the governor-general of Adana to
                            allow them to open; permission to make a tour in Syria and Adana
                            requested; copies of dispatches from Consul-General Pringle and Consul
                            Bessinger inclosed.

Protests against the decision of the United States Government;
                            reserves right to communicate further arguments under the instruction of
                            his Government.

Mr. Flourens has submitted the propositions in Department’s circular
                            of July 9, 1887, to, his colleagues, and will communicate their opinion;
                            he desires three more copies of the circular.

frus:attachment.p:
“A seal.” Supreme tribunal of justice of the State of Vera Cruz, Llave;
                            full bench, secretary’s office; this bench gave this day the following
                            sentence:

The Doctor then proceeds to bring out the second object of which I treat;
                            and I am going to copy what he has to say thereon, for it is the answer
                            to one of Mr. Bayard’s observations:

postscript.p:
That, having been likewise approved by the French Chambers, this
                                treaty was ratified by the President of that Republic on the
                                seventeenth day of the aforesaid month of March;

closer.dateline:
Sworn to and subscribed before me, a notary
                                public, September 17,
                                    1888.

frus:attachment.p:
The facts as stated in the accompanying letter, briefly summed, are as follows: These gentlemen, under the impression that the commercial agreement entered into between the United States and Spain at Madrid on the 13th February last past had for object the abolition of the different duties on the part of Spain and the retaliatory duties on the part of the United States, hitherto mutually imposed on the trade between the United States, Cuba, and Puerto Rico, and conversely between those islands and the United States, did, on the 16th of last month, ship in the port of New York, on board the American steam-ship Saratoga, bound for Havana—
No. 1–125, 125 bags of coffee;
No. 126, 161 bags of coffee;
286 bags of coffee—

